{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261104T014754Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "4a3bf4dc7b2f7e3192db0eaa", "title": "Introducing Physical-Engineering’s Last Exam for agents", "url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/introducing-physical-engineerings-last-exam-for-agents/", "source": "AI & Machine Learning", "published": "2026-11-03T08:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.97, "worthiness": 0.825, "worthiness_confidence": 0.75, "final_score": 0.825, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 4933, "delivered": true, "summary": "Google AI released Physical-Engineering's Last Exam (PLE), a benchmark with 85 long-horizon tasks across mechanical, electrical, civil, and structural engineering to evaluate AI agents on real-world engineering work. Unlike textbook benchmarks, PLE tests agents working through realistic project folders with 61 average files including CAD drawings, BIM models, and building codes, requiring production-ready deliverables that pass strict safety checks. All 14 frontier models scored below 50%, with the top performer reaching 48.3%, while only 41.3% of its designs passed all hard safety requirements. The benchmark is open at ple-bench.com with 29 public tasks and a competition leaderboard.", "jev_ms": 117.5, "jev_input_tokens": 1636, "jev_cost": 6.8712e-05, "llm_ms": 2634.0, "llm_cost": 0.002009, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261104T014754Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "8d86676710497099b1091deb", "title": "StateBench: Testing how language models track state changes over long documents", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/allenai/statebench", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-11-03T17:15:21+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.995, "worthiness_confidence": 0.98, "final_score": 0.995, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 9809, "delivered": true, "summary": "StateBench is a benchmark measuring how well language models track entity state changes across long documents, organized into four difficulty levels from simple reassignment to complex cross-variable dependencies. State tracking matters for tasks like code reading and narrative comprehension where missed updates compound errors. Testing frontier models (Gemini 3.8 Flash, Claude Opus 5.5, GPT-6 Sol/Luna) and open models reveals transformers fail on harder levels even with direct training, while Gated DeltaNet and hybrid architectures generalize far beyond training length—the key takeaway is that architecture fundamentally constrains state-tracking capability.", "jev_ms": 152.2, "jev_input_tokens": 2583, "jev_cost": 0.000108486, "llm_ms": 3177.6, "llm_cost": 0.002936, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261104T014754Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "4d3938441f216409a8f1b94c", "title": "Palette: teaching a small model to build decks in your design language", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/ibm-research/palette-deck-building", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-11-03T16:35:36+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.9575, "worthiness_confidence": 0.86, "final_score": 0.9575, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 11346, "delivered": true, "summary": "IBM Research's Palette fine-tunes a 20B open-weight model to generate presentation decks in custom design languages using synthetic training data. The system splits deck generation into two stages—a designer creates structured JSON briefs, then a coder generates slide code in parallel—mirroring how humans actually build decks. Small models struggle with layout arithmetic and multi-stage reasoning, so Palette uses a harness that checks rendered slides for overlaps and overflow, repairing broken output only when verified improvements occur. On PresentBench, Palette ranks first in Economics and Education domains, second overall. Key takeaway: decomposing complex generation tasks into verifiable substeps with reasoning-annotated training data and safety-checked repairs enables small self-hosted models to match frontier systems while maintaining design language fidelity.", "jev_ms": 155.6, "jev_input_tokens": 3093, "jev_cost": 0.000129906, "llm_ms": 2645.0, "llm_cost": 0.003694, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261104T014754Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "63945bc794809749828fed7a", "title": "CWE-bench v2: More coverage, quality, and transparency", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/cwe-bench", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-11-03T00:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.98, "worthiness": 0.75, "worthiness_confidence": 0.97, "final_score": 0.75, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 4171, "delivered": true, "summary": "CWE-bench v2 is a public benchmark of 100 security vulnerability-finding tasks covering 91 different CWEs, with deterministic programmatic scoring and a held-out evaluation set. For AI engineers building security agents, this provides a rigorous way to measure real-world vulnerability detection capabilities across diverse weakness classes. Gemini 4 Argon leads at 67.8% pass@1, but even top models fail 19% of tasks, indicating substantial room for improvement. The key takeaway: run your model directly via Harbor from Hugging Face with `harbor run` to get reproducible, cost-comparable results without manual setup.", "jev_ms": 154.5, "jev_input_tokens": 1600, "jev_cost": 6.72e-05, "llm_ms": 2296.5, "llm_cost": 0.001952, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261104T014754Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "a6f54d325d31634f891659b5", "title": "Transformers Model Integration Contracts", "url": "https://huggingface.co/blog/transformers-mic", "source": "Hugging Face - Blog", "published": "2026-11-03T00:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.9275, "worthiness_confidence": 0.77, "final_score": 0.9275, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 9720, "delivered": true, "summary": "Hugging Face introduced Model Integration Contracts (MICs), a testing standard for catching regressions in Transformers models before they reach main. MICs combine shrunk model fixtures (e.g., 66 MB instead of 1,227 MB), runnable usage examples, and baseline outputs to detect bugs like the T5 SDPA attention regression that leaked for two months. The approach uses CPU-based fast integration tests on PRs while preserving real preprocessing and layer diversity, with an LLM-assisted CLI tool (tmic) for building contracts. This matters because testing all 750+ model types on real hardware is impractical, but small fixtures catch real bugs efficiently without blocking PRs yet.", "jev_ms": 169.6, "jev_input_tokens": 2754, "jev_cost": 0.000115668, "llm_ms": 2550.9, "llm_cost": 0.003249, "errors": []}
